WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:12.869
Your scheduler must always run the most urgent task next, even as new tasks arrive every millisecond. Sorting everything each time is too slow. A heap is the answer.

00:00:14.069 --> 00:00:14.998
Pause and think.

00:00:15.398 --> 00:00:20.344
To keep the k largest items from a stream, do you use a min-heap or a max-heap?

00:00:20.744 --> 00:00:28.058
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:29.258 --> 00:00:35.818
A heap gives you the smallest, or the largest, item instantly. It powers schedulers and top k problems.

00:00:35.841 --> 00:00:46.139
New word: scheduler. The OS part that decides which task runs on which CPU and when. Picture it like this: A teacher deciding whose turn it is.

00:00:46.539 --> 00:00:58.915
New word: heap. A tree-shaped structure that always gives you the smallest (or largest) item quickly; also the dynamic memory area. Picture it like this: A tournament where the champion is always on top.

00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:03.566
Think of a hospital emergency room.

00:01:04.016 --> 00:01:08.788
Patients do not wait in arrival order. The most urgent patient is always seen next.

00:01:09.238 --> 00:01:14.532
The triage nurse does not fully sort everyone, they just make sure the most urgent person is at the front.

00:01:14.982 --> 00:01:19.359
That partial order is cheap to maintain, and that is the idea of a heap.

00:01:20.709 --> 00:01:23.577
A min heap is a tree stored inside an array.

00:01:23.615 --> 00:01:32.055
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:01:33.665 --> 00:01:40.572
The rule: every parent is smaller than or equal to its children. So the smallest is always at index zero, the root.

00:01:41.022 --> 00:01:46.920
The children of index i live at two i plus one and two i plus two. No pointers needed.

00:01:47.048 --> 00:01:55.407
New word: pointer. A variable holding the memory address of something else. Picture it like this: A note saying "the book is on shelf 4".

00:01:57.957 --> 00:02:00.697
To insert zero, place it at the end of the array.

00:02:01.147 --> 00:02:04.967
Then sift it up: while it is smaller than its parent, swap them.

00:02:05.417 --> 00:02:09.121
It swaps with two, then with one, and becomes the new root.

00:02:09.571 --> 00:02:13.181
At most log n swaps, because the tree is balanced.

00:02:14.531 --> 00:02:16.064
Here is a top k trick.

00:02:16.514 --> 00:02:20.333
To find the second largest number, keep a min heap of size two.

00:02:20.783 --> 00:02:25.636
Every new number goes in. If the heap grows past two, pop the smallest.

00:02:26.086 --> 00:02:33.087
At the end, the top of the heap is the answer. This costs n log k, great for streams of latency samples.

00:02:33.107 --> 00:02:41.571
New word: latency. How long one operation takes from request to answer. Picture it like this: How long you wait for your coffee.

00:02:44.121 --> 00:02:50.379
In C plus plus, priority queue is a max heap by default. Pass greater to make it a min heap.

00:02:50.521 --> 00:02:56.825
New word: queue. First-in-first-out structure. Picture it like this: A line at a bus stop.

00:02:58.471 --> 00:03:02.000
In Java, PriorityQueue is a min heap by default.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:08.940
In C sharp, PriorityQueue takes an element and a priority, and dequeues the lowest priority first.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:14.058
Dijkstra's shortest path algorithm and OS schedulers both rely on heaps.

00:03:14.104 --> 00:03:27.200
New word: O S. Operating System: the core software (like Linux or Android) that manages hardware and runs apps. Picture it like this: The building manager who hands out rooms, power and water to tenants.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.421
Interview tips for heaps.

00:03:31.871 --> 00:03:38.698
Say which way the heap points and why. For the k largest, you want a min heap, so you can evict the smallest.

00:03:39.148 --> 00:03:43.618
Know that building a heap from an array takes only O of n, not n log n.

00:03:44.068 --> 00:03:48.689
And mention quickselect as an alternative for one-off kth element queries.

00:03:50.039 --> 00:03:55.054
Whenever you hear top k, smallest so far, or next event, think heap.

00:03:55.044 --> 00:03:56.298
Back to the question.

00:03:56.698 --> 00:04:01.644
To keep the k largest items from a stream, do you use a min-heap or a max-heap?

00:04:02.044 --> 00:04:07.489
A min-heap of size k: the smallest of the top k sits on top, ready to be kicked out.

00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:09.560
Quick recap.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.723
A heap always exposes the min or max.

00:04:13.123 --> 00:04:15.282
Push and pop are O of log n.

00:04:15.682 --> 00:04:18.225
Top k uses a heap of size k.
